---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import Gloss from '../../components/Gloss.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { line } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const tfr = assertion('more-children-per-woman-than-ohio')
const repl = assertion('below-replacement-eleven-years-after-ohio')
const pre = assertion('prematurity-fell-and-birthweight-did-not')
const im = assertion('infant-mortality-halved-then-stopped')
const bad = assertion('twenty-twenty-one-is-the-worst-year')
const ovl = assertion('the-overlap-says-it-is-the-denominator')
const few = assertion('fewer-patients-about-as-long')
const outp = assertion('the-work-went-outpatient')
const occA = assertion('occupancy-did-not-move-because-the-beds-came-out')
const blu = assertion('bluffton-has-stopped-taking-inpatients')
const kin = assertion('the-long-term-hospital-emptied-too')
const two = assertion('the-two-big-hospitals-fell-in-proportion')

const tYears = [
  2000, 2001, 2002, 2003, 2004, 2005, 2006, 2007, 2008, 2009, 2010, 2011, 2012, 2013, 2014, 2015,
  2016, 2017, 2018, 2019, 2020,
]
const tfAllen = [
  2360.7, 2358.3, 2229.0, 2281.7, 2210.4, 2208.1, 2213.0, 2218.5, 2147.8, 2177.9, 2082.1, 2030.4,
  2048.5, 2036.1, 2050.1, 2072.3, 2049.3, 2031.0, 2063.7, 2083.5, 1954.2,
]
const tfOhio = [
  2020.4, 1982.3, 1955.6, 1974.7, 1975.4, 1978.8, 2021.1, 2034.1, 2013.9, 1967.3, 1897.1, 1879.4,
  1884.5, 1879.9, 1878.0, 1869.1, 1846.0, 1821.0, 1793.6, 1780.1, 1712.4,
]
const iYears = [
  2004, 2005, 2006, 2007, 2008, 2009, 2010, 2011, 2012, 2013, 2014, 2015, 2016, 2017, 2018, 2019,
  2020,
]
const imAllen = [
  9.75, 10.33, 10.14, 9.67, 8.72, 8.35, 7.17, 6.58, 7.34, 6.37, 6.62, 6.79, 7.74, 7.45, 8.11, 7.47,
  6.95,
]
const imOhio = [
  7.7, 7.81, 7.83, 7.79, 7.78, 7.82, 7.72, 7.76, 7.71, 7.64, 7.47, 7.36, 7.26, 7.2, 7.12, 7.13,
  7.03,
]
const hYears = [2011, 2012, 2013, 2014, 2015, 2016, 2017, 2018, 2019, 2020, 2021, 2022, 2023]
const disch = [
  26924, 27662, 26369, 25048, 26149, 24652, 25293, 25127, 25089, 22077, 22582, 22019, 22858,
]
const outShare = [46.9, 48.5, 48.9, 50.1, 49.2, 50.4, 50.3, 51.0, 52.7, 50.2, 51.7, 53.9, 55.3]
const occ = [55.6, 53.5, 51.9, 50.8, 55.6, 55.8, 55.0, 52.7, 58.3, 55.7, 59.8, 57.6, 56.0]

const tfSpec = line(
  tYears,
  [
    { name: 'Allen County', y: tfAllen },
    { name: 'Ohio', y: tfOhio },
  ],
  { yTitle: 'Births per 1,000 women', labelLast: true },
)
const imSpec = line(
  iYears,
  [
    { name: 'Allen County', y: imAllen },
    { name: 'Ohio', y: imOhio },
  ],
  { yTitle: 'Infant deaths per 1,000 live births, five-year rate', labelLast: true },
)
const hSpec = line(hYears, [{ name: 'Discharges', y: disch }], {
  yTitle: 'Inpatient discharges, all five hospitals',
})
const oSpec = line(
  hYears,
  [
    { name: 'Outpatient share of charges', y: outShare },
    { name: 'Occupancy', y: occ },
  ],
  { yTitle: 'Per cent', tickSuffix: '%', labelLast: true },
)
---

<Article slug="more-children-and-fewer-patients">
  <p>
    Two questions a county reference should be able to answer are what happens when a child is born
    here and what the hospitals do. Neither was answerable from this one: a birth count with no
    outcome attached to it, and five hospitals known by their bed counts and their payrolls.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={tfr} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="tfr"
    title="More children per woman than the state, every year"
    caption="Total fertility rate for Allen County and for Ohio, births per 1,000 women. The county's line is above the state's in all twenty-one comparable years, and both fall by about the same proportion."
    columns={['Year', 'Allen County', 'Ohio']}
    rows={tYears.map((y, i) => [y, tfAllen[i], tfOhio[i]])}
    height={340}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="tfr" set:html={JSON.stringify(tfSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={repl} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={pre} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={im} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="im"
    title="Infant mortality, halved and then flat"
    caption="Five-year infant mortality rate, labelled by the last year of each window. The county ran well above Ohio for a decade, crossed below it around 2011, and has been at it since."
    columns={['Window ending', 'Allen County', 'Ohio']}
    rows={iYears.map((y, i) => [y, imAllen[i], imOhio[i]])}
    height={320}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="im" set:html={JSON.stringify(imSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={bad} />

  <h2>A break the publisher marked</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={ovl} />
  <Gloss>
    Three of these measures are published twice — once through 2020 and once from 2018 — and the
    three years they share are given away free. Everything counted off a birth certificate is
    identical to the digit across the break. The one quantity that divides births by a count of
    women differs in all three years, by 0.05 per cent in the first and 0.59 in the last, and Ohio's
    rows do the same thing. So the break is in the population estimate underneath the rate and not
    in the vital statistics on top of it, the birth measures are one series across twenty-two years,
    and the fertility rate is honestly two. None of that is written down anywhere; it is what the
    overlap says, and the overlap is usually deleted as a duplicate.
  </Gloss>

  <h2>What the hospitals did</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={few} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="disch"
    title="Fifteen per cent fewer people admitted"
    caption="Inpatient discharges from all five Allen County hospitals, every fiscal year from 2011. The average stay over the same period went from 4.37 days to 4.49 — up, not down."
    columns={['Fiscal year', 'Discharges']}
    rows={hYears.map((y, i) => [y, disch[i]])}
    height={300}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="disch" set:html={JSON.stringify(hSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={outp} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={occA} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="oshare"
    title="The outpatient share moved and occupancy did not"
    caption="Outpatient charges as a share of all hospital charges in Allen County, beside the occupancy of the county's hospital beds. Charges are list prices; the share is the finding."
    columns={['Fiscal year', 'Outpatient share', 'Occupancy']}
    rows={hYears.map((y, i) => [y, `${outShare[i]}%`, `${occ[i]}%`])}
    height={320}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="oshare" set:html={JSON.stringify(oSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Which is why a bed count is a poor thing to read alone. The county lost 78 beds and 4,066
    admissions across the same thirteen years, and the two fell at close enough to the same rate that
    the share of beds occupied on an average night is where it was.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={blu} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={kin} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={two} />
</Article>
